WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.290
The fascinating thing that I've always found
about physics is that it studies collections

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:09.000
of objects and what they do together.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.720
Like water can suddenly freeze, or water can
boil.

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:16.810
Now, that's really interesting.

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:20.560
And you think that that has nothing to do
with people, other than that people are studying it.

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:23.880
But people are like particles.

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:25.650
People get together on a road.

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:30.199
They form traffic jams, which is like something
solidifying.

00:00:30.199 --> 00:00:35.440
They get together into groups, which is very
much like particles do.

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:40.240
So the question arises: Can you ever come
up with a kind of mathematics that is like

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:44.690
the mathematics used for physics, but describes
collections of people?

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:48.390
Now, immediately, there's the objection:
Wait a minute!

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:51.990
People have free will, and people are very
different.

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:53.010
We're all very different.

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:54.780
We're not like particles.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:58.880
But something interesting happens because
when we have lots of objects, it doesn't really

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:04.000
matter that one or two or three or all of
them are individually free-willed.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:07.360
Collectively, they will act together as a
group.

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:10.160
And that group has actually some predicability.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:11.160
It's very interesting.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:17.580
If I think of these coins, if I flip a coin,
I'll get heads or tails with 50 percent probability.

00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:23.850
So the chance of me flipping say 10 coins
and getting 10 heads is — well, it's just

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:25.480
really unlikely.

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:30.920
But when it comes to people, the chances of
having people all suddenly appear on the road

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:34.819
at the same time of the day and cause a traffic
jam is actually quite predictable.

00:01:34.819 --> 00:01:38.100
It's also the same with, for example, stock
markets.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:44.299
Stock markets crash, and they rise again much
quicker and much more often than if you were

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:46.600
just flipping coins.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:50.280
And so the big question arises:

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:58.639
Can we describe how people act in groups,
say when they're doing extreme things?

00:01:58.639 --> 00:02:03.390
Can we describe that using the same kind of
mathematics?

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:05.429
And it turns out that we can.

00:02:05.429 --> 00:02:10.250
Collectively, people behave in quite predictable
ways.

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:15.731
And one of the things that we've been particularly
looking at in the last couple of years, and

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:20.660
it's now becoming a very important topic,
is: How could people do that online?

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:22.920
Online, people are freer.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:27.890
They're not kind of bound by who they live
next to or who their immediate friends are

00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:28.890
in work.

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:30.080
They're free to explore.

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:32.049
And what you see are patterns.

00:02:32.049 --> 00:02:37.500
Very definite patterns appear in a way that
you don't actually see in the real world,

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:39.629
but it happens in the online world.

00:02:39.629 --> 00:02:44.190
The online world where we do worry about what
people do collectively.

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:47.079
We do worry about extreme groups.

00:02:47.079 --> 00:02:51.049
That's where the mathematics can actually
be more powerful, and that's what we've been

00:02:51.049 --> 00:02:51.549
looking at.

